1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,468
Welcome to Episode Five, an extended lesson on Kill Zones. Now we apply the schedule to price.

2
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:16,480
We will walk through the Asian, London and New York sessions, map their ranges, and study what happens when the textbook pattern does not appear.

3
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:21,916
All charts use New York time and the CMI reference schedule from Episode Four.

4
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:31,368
The Asian session. In the CMI schedule, the Asian Kill Zone runs from eight p m to midnight, New York time.

5
00:00:31,788 --> 00:00:38,052
For many forex pairs and US index futures, this is often a quieter, range-bound period.

6
00:00:38,472 --> 00:00:44,328
We mark the Asian high and Asian low: the extremes printed inside the window only.

7
00:00:44,748 --> 00:00:50,676
Stops may rest just beyond them, so they become potential buy-side and sell-side liquidity.

8
00:00:51,096 --> 00:00:55,416
Later sessions may interact with these levels. May, not must.

9
00:00:57,236 --> 00:01:03,620
London. In a bullish scenario, price trades below the Asian low early in the London window.

10
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:09,656
The sweep is followed by a reclaim and an expansion higher. London then leaves its own high.

11
00:01:10,076 --> 00:01:14,444
That London high and London low become new references for New York.

12
00:01:14,864 --> 00:01:20,696
The bearish scenario is the mirror. London takes the Asian high, then expands lower.

13
00:01:21,116 --> 00:01:27,620
In both cases, an early London extreme can turn out to be the high or low of the day. But not always.

14
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:35,192
London may reverse after taking an Asian extreme, or simply continue through it. Read the reaction, not the clock.

15
00:01:37,012 --> 00:01:40,396
The New York morning. We bring in the London high and low.

16
00:01:40,816 --> 00:01:45,232
And the previous day high and low, which are higher-timeframe references.

17
00:01:45,652 --> 00:01:49,420
New York may continue London’s direction, or reverse it.

18
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:58,864
Many scheduled US economic releases come out at eight thirty New York time, inside this window. Volatility often rises around them.

19
00:01:59,284 --> 00:02:06,964
In this example, New York continues London’s bullish move, takes the London high, and sweeps the previous day high.

20
00:02:07,384 --> 00:02:13,816
After that, price pulls back. Whether that becomes a reversal is something only later price can show.

21
00:02:15,636 --> 00:02:22,788
Around New York lunch, from noon to one, participation often reduces and price commonly consolidates.

22
00:02:23,208 --> 00:02:26,760
So breakouts during lunch are usually treated with caution.

23
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:32,628
In the afternoon window, from one thirty to four, price may continue the morning move.

24
00:02:33,048 --> 00:02:36,936
Or it may reverse into the close, as positions are squared.

25
00:02:37,356 --> 00:02:40,932
The lesson: time alone does not determine direction.

26
00:02:42,752 --> 00:02:46,808
Now we map the whole day. Start with the three session windows.

27
00:02:47,228 --> 00:02:49,124
Mark the Asian high and low.

28
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:51,440
Mark the London high and low.

29
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:53,948
Mark the New York morning range.

30
00:02:54,368 --> 00:02:56,408
And the previous day high and low.

31
00:02:56,828 --> 00:03:06,476
Each session’s extremes become reference levels and potential liquidity for the next. This map turns time into price references you can actually use.

32
00:03:08,296 --> 00:03:15,616
Session models fail often, so let’s study the failures. Failure one: London never sweeps the Asian low.

33
00:03:16,036 --> 00:03:23,236
It simply breaks the Asian high and trends higher all day. Anyone waiting for a sweep of the low misses the move.

34
00:03:23,656 --> 00:03:27,832
Failure two: London breaks the Asian low and just keeps going.

35
00:03:28,252 --> 00:03:33,748
There is no reclaim. The break was continuation, and New York continues it as well.

36
00:03:34,168 --> 00:03:40,648
In both cases, the correct response is to observe the actual chart, not to force a session model.

37
00:03:42,468 --> 00:03:48,012
Failure three: London rallies, and New York reverses the entire London move.

38
00:03:48,432 --> 00:03:53,064
A trader who assumed New York would continue London is now on the wrong side.

39
00:03:53,484 --> 00:04:01,164
Failure four: a news release at eight thirty creates one abnormal candle that sweeps both sides of the range.

40
00:04:01,584 --> 00:04:07,464
Sweeps caused by news spikes are not clean model evidence. Treat them with extra caution.

41
00:04:07,884 --> 00:04:14,892
Failure five: the Kill Zones open and close, and nothing happens. No sweep, no displacement.

42
00:04:15,312 --> 00:04:18,672
That happens more often than social media suggests.

43
00:04:19,092 --> 00:04:24,156
When the session gives no clear evidence, no trade is a valid conclusion.

44
00:04:25,976 --> 00:04:32,192
To summarise. The Asian session often forms a range whose extremes become references.

45
00:04:32,612 --> 00:04:42,068
London may sweep an Asian extreme or continue through it. New York interacts with London’s range, the previous day’s levels, and scheduled news.

46
00:04:42,488 --> 00:04:44,768
Map every session’s high and low.

47
00:04:45,188 --> 00:04:49,364
And remember that failed session patterns are normal. Do not force them.

48
